No. English law recognises no general duty of good faith in contract.
- Walford v Miles [1992] 2 AC 128 (HL) held that an agreement to negotiate in good faith is unenforceable.
- Interfoto Picture Library Ltd v Stiletto Visual Programmes Ltd [1989] QB 433 (CA) shows the approach English law takes instead, through specific doctrines.

Good faith can arise in some cases:
- by an express term (Mid Essex Hospital Services NHS Trust v Compass Group UK and Ireland Ltd [2013] EWCA Civ 200);
- as an implied term in "relational" contracts (Yam Seng Pte Ltd v International Trade Corp Ltd [2013] EWHC 111 (QB), [2013] 1 Lloyd's Rep 526; Bates v Post Office Ltd (No 3) [2019] EWHC 606 (QB));
- through limits on the exercise of contractual discretion (Braganza v BP Shipping Ltd [2015] UKSC 17, [2015] 1 WLR 1661);
- in particular categories such as insurance, partnership and fiduciary relationships, and under consumer law.

The court adopted a "range of factors" or "trio of considerations" test (Lord Toulson):
1. the underlying purpose of the prohibition that was broken, and whether denying the claim would advance it;
2. any other relevant public policies that denying the claim might affect;
3. whether denying the claim would be a proportionate response to the illegality.

It replaced the reliance test in Tinsley v Milligan [1994] 1 AC 340 and the rule-based approaches built on it. Patel v Mirza [2016] UKSC 42, [2017] AC 467. The test was applied in Henderson v Dorset Healthcare University NHS Foundation Trust [2020] UKSC 43, [2021] AC 563.
